{"qid":"Q888574","names":["Pagan Kingdom"],"title":"Pagan Kingdom","endonym":{"native":"ပုဂံခေတ်","lang":"my"},"capsule":"The Pagan Kingdom (Bagan) was the first Burmese empire, unified by King Anawrahta (r.1044–1077), who conquered the Mon kingdom of Thaton in 1057 and introduced Theravada Buddhism, Pali literacy, and Mon administrative practices to the Irrawaddy basin. At its height under Kyansittha (r.1084–1113) and Alaungsithu (r.1113–1167) the kingdom commanded the full Irrawaddy valley from the dry zone capital of Pagan — whose more than 3,000 surviving temples rank it among the great monument complexes of Southeast Asia. Mongol invasions under Kublai Khan beginning in 1277 (Battle of Ngasaunggyan) progressively dismembered Pagan authority; the last king fled the capital in 1287, and by 1293 the kingdom had fragmented into Shan and Mon successor states.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Pagan's territorial extent is attested by stone inscriptions, temple dedications, and Chinese tribute records for the core Irrawaddy valley; frontier zones with the Shan hills and coastal Mon areas are conjectured and less reliable.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Pagan kingdom","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Pagan-kingdom"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Pagan Kingdom","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pagan_Kingdom"}],"britannica":"place/Pagan-kingdom","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Pagan Kingdom","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Thamoddarit","nativeName":"သမောဒ္ဒရာဇ်","roman":"Thamoaddarit","start":107,"end":152,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Yatheinkha","nativeN